Inheritance and Polymorphism in cpp
  Inheritance:
    A child class can take features of their parent class
  Real-life example
    Think about a father and a son.
      - Father has a house.
      - Father has a car.
      - Son can inherit these properties.
  Types of inheritance in C++
There are five main types.
1. Single inheritance
One parent class → One child class.
Example: Father → Son.
2. Multilevel inheritance
One class inherits another, and a third class inherits the second.
Example: Grandfather → Father → Son.
3. Hierarchical inheritance
Multiple child classes inherit one parent class.
Example: Father → Son and Daughter.
4. Multiple inheritance
One child class inherits from multiple parent classes.
Example: Child inherits from Father and Mother.
5. Hybrid inheritance
Combination of two or more types of inheritance.
  Polymorphism:
    one function in many forms like add(int a, int b),add(int a, int b, int c)
  Polymorphism means one name having many forms.
    In simple words:
      The same function or operation can perform different tasks depending on the situation.
    Real-life example
    Think about a person.
      The same person can act as:
        - A student in college.
        - A son at home.
        - A friend with friends.
  1. Compile-time polymorphism (Static polymorphism).
  2. Run-time polymorphism (Dynamic polymorphism).
A. Compile-time polymorphism
In compile-time polymorphism, the compiler decides which function to execute during compilation.

Two common techniques:
- Function overloading.
- Operator overloading.
B. Run-time polymorphism
In run-time polymorphism, the function to execute is decided while the program is running.

It is commonly achieved using:
- Function overriding.
- Virtual functions.
//Inheritance and Polymorphism

  
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Animal {
public:
    virtual void Sound() {
        cout << "Animal makes a sound" << endl;
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    void Sound() override {
        cout << "Bow Bow" << endl;
    }
};

class Cat : public Animal {
public:
    void Sound() override {
        cout << "Meow Meow" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {

    Dog d;
    Cat c;

    d.Sound();
    c.Sound();

    return 0;
}
//OUTPUT
Bow Bow
Meow Meow
